#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571489} :activity/id #uuid "56fd0998-bb06-5998-a60b-520c9c7f0ced" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048393 :topic/id #uuid "9babae1d-ca0d-5438-a2e4-a65dbba9f7ef" :topic/math-academy-id 1913} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571467 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "327c20aa-5b6d-5d5d-95d5-67fe1edd6dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244765} {:answer/id #uuid "33ffc812-4891-586d-bc0d-281bd530059e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244781} {:answer/id #uuid "be00ba0f-33e3-51bb-b788-c863cec54aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246007} {:answer/id #uuid "beae8b6f-7eef-5f2b-b111-68f42f84b6fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246016} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a13eed-4a8b-5532-9d37-57dbd1d42511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246357}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246016} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb514e9d-3fa7-5bab-af7e-44674ca75bc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cac3744a-0cdf-56d6-88ab-9bba2eefa254" :question/math-academy-id "q-111192" :question/problem "A particular town has a fleet of $720$ bicycles for rent. The table below gives the pattern of rental and return locations over a typical day. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Museum** | **Station** | **Gym** |\n| To: | **Museum** | $0.9$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| **Station** | $0$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |  |\n| **Gym** | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nFor example, $40\\%$ of the bicycles at the Station are moved to the Gym every day.\n\nHow many bicycles would eventually be located at the Station if the listed probabilities remained constant over many days?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.3 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.3 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & -0.7 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & -0.8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.3 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.7 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & -0.8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}+3({x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 6{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many days, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the bicycles will be at the station. Therefore, the total number of bikes at the station after many days will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 720=120.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "21f93e1c-b14b-5b4b-9122-02e68376a76a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571480}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571478 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b828146-3af8-5909-903e-b51a9b905309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266379} {:answer/id #uuid "783f6a21-5d08-51b0-a129-e4614e791183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267200} {:answer/id #uuid "8d08161d-17c7-50f2-91f4-495659a8a63b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267404} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ca4cde-be3a-5390-b261-7492120df2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268349} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1eb026-f8a8-5839-b850-5db4012183c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268382}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268349} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e83eae8-e4dc-5643-b310-0c6ec7afc244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d5136e32-dc7f-55de-b02b-4d944cd2c122" :question/math-academy-id "q-135323" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.75 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.75 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.75 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0.75{x}_{1}+0.6{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 75{x}_{1}-60{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{5}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{5}{9}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{4}{9}-\\frac{5}{9}=-\\frac{1}{9}.$"} :step/id #uuid "432ba657-a362-5709-a218-2f2609d159ce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571480 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571380 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6c4c14-fa02-5f31-bd54-0657abba4c2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570907} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef6cbc2-9527-5c4a-b3fb-af45a02579cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570934} {:answer/id #uuid "2fcc368e-ff7d-583c-987f-3674a47ece51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570963} {:answer/id #uuid "96526529-e366-5b8c-bc8b-f39d7129270f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571128} {:answer/id #uuid "a13ecaef-26d3-57e0-b3ba-3271c0005758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571141}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570934} :answer-field/id #uuid "55d9c9a4-bfde-5bd4-b59e-36fd1d241250" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2001aca6-021f-51d2-9ef2-92bba5a3a5a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-135313" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.45 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.25 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{18}{65} \\\\ \\frac{30}{65} \\\\ \\frac{17}{65}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $q$ is a stochastic vector\n\nII. $Pq=q$\n\nIII. $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.45 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.25 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{18}{65} \\\\ \\frac{30}{65} \\\\ \\frac{17}{65}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{65}+\\frac{9}{65}+0 \\\\ \\frac{6.3}{65}+\\frac{13.5}{65}+\\frac{10.2}{65} \\\\ \\frac{2.7}{65}+\\frac{7.5}{65}+\\frac{6.8}{65}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{18}{65} \\\\ \\frac{30}{65} \\\\ \\frac{17}{65}\\end{bmatrix}=q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector such that $Pq=q.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "50cc8199-756e-5cf4-addb-4f2e494fb41f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571478}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571489 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "864ade46-01a0-5dbe-aaeb-70b080238c2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121236} {:answer/id #uuid "91c60bcf-63de-5061-a278-e0de92932ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121237} {:answer/id #uuid "92954c49-8e23-52ad-a533-209d6f104703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121238} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7fa6cd-38ab-5343-80a2-d83135dd32a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121239} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff76532-db6a-5ad9-bd52-d3ab3ac2e98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121240}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9b3ab77a-f24c-5f05-9941-6e64bc0c5347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9d9e86d2-c897-5b07-851a-148dd18a6f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110243" :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.6 & 0.6\\end{bmatrix}, q = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a + b - c$?"} :step/id #uuid "7950fcc1-0b95-59d2-b7e9-f51dd6540cc1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571467}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269057 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ba600a6-1907-5e14-b550-b044551ef5e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "65\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266530} {:answer/id #uuid "33aab18e-ac2a-5fe2-8c2c-6bbd3d17578b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "85\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266597} {:answer/id #uuid "656ef5ce-de1c-585b-b58e-bcb0488a1f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267042} {:answer/id #uuid "78a20fcd-e709-5231-beab-5b7abd75b2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267205} {:answer/id #uuid "84bfcca2-04fa-5a72-b5ec-b6d4efc25021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "75\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267313} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e7c105c-3db3-51ac-83dc-0a87716a4e48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b9da89ba-6ad8-53a7-ac87-9c6b7aab9eb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-135409" :question/problem "A factory produces two types of sugar: brown and extra-refined. Every year, the factory's sales executives study how customers' preferences change between the two types of sugar. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Brown** | **Extra-Refined** |\n| To: | **Brown** | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n| **Extra-Refined** | $0.2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nIt's found that $20\\%$ of customers who currently buy brown sugar will switch to extra-refined sugar next year, while $100\\%$ of customers who currently purchase extra-refined sugar will switch to brown sugar next year.\n\nAssuming that there are $450\\,000$ customers in total and that the given probabilities remain constant over many years, how many customers will eventually consume extra-refined sugar?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n{P}^{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.84 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.16 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=5{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ 6{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the customers will eventually use extra-refined sugar. Therefore, the number of customers who will eventually use this type of sugar is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 450\\,000=75\\,000.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c7a9659a-3fe4-58a1-b326-0dbab19d4e9b"}] :activity/title "Steady-State Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570882}]}
